@begin(section)

@title(Architecture)

This article describes the overarching architecture of dsolv, together with
some explanation about some of the design decisions. dsolv is a port of the
degasolv dependency resolver from Clojure to Common Lisp; the architecture
described here is inherited from degasolv.

@begin(section)
@title(Background)

At one of my previous jobs, I was a Build Engineer — a person who built the
code that the developers wrote and made it available. I had @i(lots) of
dependency problems coming at me from all different sides:

@begin(list)
@item(One module in language A depending on another from language B)
@item(Developers working with a language (at the time) with no clear package
manager ecosystem (**cough** C++ **cough**))
@item(Package manager developers breaking builds with backwards-incompatible
behavior)
@item(A dependency graph that looked like a dream catcher)
@end(list)

So I decided to build a tool that would do these things:

@begin(list)
@item(Resolve dependencies the right way, safely)
@item(Even resolve dependency chains for different package systems (apt, pip,
java))
@item(Be super versatile and generic, able to be plugged into an arbitrary
build script)
@end(list)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Core Resolver)

At the core of dsolv is a monster function called
@c(resolve-dependencies-deluxe). It is a rather large function with a
backtracking, SAT-solver-ish design. Originally it was written to have a
conflict strategy of @c(exclusive) and a resolve strategy of @c(thorough)
hard-coded. In other words, the "first class" original use case of dsolv was
a SAT-solver-class dependency resolver that only allowed a single version of
any dependency, and ensured that all parties depending on that dependency had
a chance to agree on what was chosen. These options were later added to allow
dsolv to act more like Maven and give any build engineer using dsolv useful
"handbrakes" to change how resolution was being done in-house so that it
could be modified to conform to business needs. Other options, such as the
list strategy and the search strategy, were added as time progressed as well
for similar reasons, and also, frankly, to fix bugs (behaviors that were
never originally intended).

dsolv models a dependency resolution problem with three main data
structures, all built on @link[uri="https://fset.common-lisp.dev/"](FSet)
persistent data structures:

@begin(deflist)
@term(@c(version-predicate))
@def(A relation (such as @c(greater-than) or @c(less-equal)) paired with a
version string.)
@term(@c(requirement))
@def(A status (@c(present) or @c(absent)), a package id, and a set of
alternative version predicates (the "spec").)
@term(@c(package-info))
@def(A package id, version, location, and requirements, as read from a card
or a repository index.)
@end(deflist)

Requirements are parsed from strings such as @c(a>2.0,<3.0;>4.0) or @c(!b)
by @c(string-to-requirement).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Package Systems)

dsolv resolves dependencies from "package systems" that know how to slurp
repositories into query functions:

@begin(deflist)
@term(@c(degasolv))
@def(The default package system. Reads @c(.dscard) files and
@c(.dsrepo) repository indexes, using NRDL as the data format.)
@term(@c(apt))
@def(Slurps Debian-style @c(Packages.gz) indexes. The parser deliberately
avoids regular expressions: records are cut at blank lines and key/value
pairs are split at the first colon using simple linear string operations.
Regular expressions can be NP-hard to parse and made the original degasolv
take about 40 minutes on the full Ubuntu package listing, so the parsing here
is kept linear and predictable.)
@term(@c(git))
@def(A stub, preserved for compatibility with degasolv's package system
interface. It returns no packages.)
@term(@c(subproc))
@def(Runs an external executable, parses its JSON or NRDL output, and
converts it into resolver query functions. Configure it with
@c(-x)/@c(--subproc-exe) and @c(-u)/@c(--subproc-output-format).)
@end(deflist)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(CLI and Configuration)

The command line interface is built with
@link[uri="https://github.com/djha-skin/cliff"](CLIFF). CLIFF gathers options
from configuration files (NRDL or JSON), environment variables, and the
command line, merging them into a single options table. dsolv adds
degasolv-compatible command line aliases and @c(DEGASOLV_*) environment
variable aliases on top of CLIFF's own conventions; see the
@c(Command Reference) for details.

@end(section)

@end(section)
